# Appendix B: Post-Scam Recovery Playbook {-}

If something in this book has happened to you, this appendix is where you start. The actions below are organized by urgency, not by severity. Even if you are not sure yet how serious the scam was, working through the first two timelines will put you in the best possible position to recover your money, your evidence, and your peace of mind.

## In the first fifteen minutes

Leave the venue. If you are physically inside a place where you were scammed — a {list typical country-specific venues: gem shop, carpet shop, tea house, tailor, tour-operator desk, bar with a suddenly inflated bill} — do not argue, do not try to negotiate, and do not pay with a second form of payment.

Do not sign anything, including receipts. A signed credit-card slip is much harder to dispute than one you refused to sign. If staff are blocking your exit, dial **{EMERGENCY-NUMBER}** ({POLICE-FORCE-NAME}). {English-availability note.}

Screenshot everything you can, as discreetly as possible: the bill, the sign outside the venue, the street intersection, the shop's business-license certificate ({country-specific legal name for this document}), the card terminal, and the face of the person who served you if you can do it without escalating.

If your bag or wallet was just stolen in the street, shout "{local-word-for-thief}" — {local bystanders' typical response}.

## Within the first hour

Walk to the nearest **{tourist-police-branch-or-police-station}**. Say "{local-phrase-for-I-want-to-file-a-report}." You will walk out with a {country-specific legal name for police report — e.g., *denuncia*, *şikayet tutanağı*, *bai jaeng kwaam*}. This is the document your credit-card issuer and travel insurer will demand.

{OPTIONAL: mention any online pre-filing portal the country has — denuncias.policia.es (Spain), policia.ru (Russia), etc.}

Freeze the card that was used. International emergency lines for the major card issuers are listed in Appendix C.

## Within the first twenty-four hours

File a chargeback with your card issuer. Use the {country-specific report-name} reference number as documentation. Chargebacks initiated within twenty-four hours have a significantly higher success rate than those filed a week later.

Call **{TOURIST-HELP-HOTLINE-NUMBER}** ({TOURIST-HELP-AGENCY-NAME}) for advice on next steps if you are not sure how to escalate.

If your passport was photographed, held, or taken, notify your embassy or consulate immediately.

Email your travel-insurance provider with a summary, timestamps, the police-report reference, and photographs of the bill.

If the scam involved an off-platform rental or a wire-fraud hotel booking, open the dispute on the booking platform as well.

## Within the first week

File a formal consumer-protection complaint with the **{COUNTRY-CONSUMER-PROTECTION-AGENCY}** at **{CONSUMER-HOTLINE}** or **{CONSUMER-WEBSITE}**.

For **transport overcharging** — licensed taxis, ride-hail, tour minibuses — file a parallel complaint with the municipal transportation authority.

For **banking and payment disputes** — escalate to the **{COUNTRY-CENTRAL-BANK-OR-FINANCIAL-REGULATOR}**.

Submit the full travel-insurance claim with all evidence gathered so far.

Consider leaving a neutral, factual review on Google Maps or TripAdvisor. Do not name individual staff members; describe the business by its official name only. {COUNTRY} defamation law is {strict/moderate} — stick to verifiable facts.

## If your phone or SIM was stolen

Call your carrier immediately to suspend the line. The major {COUNTRY-ADJECTIVE} carriers:
- **{CARRIER-1}** — dial **{SHORT-CODE}** or +{COUNTRY-CODE} {international-line}
- **{CARRIER-2}** — dial **{SHORT-CODE}**
- **{CARRIER-3}** — dial **{SHORT-CODE}**

If you are on a tourist eSIM (Airalo, Saily, Holafly), the app will let you suspend the line in a few taps.

File a police report including your phone's **IMEI** number. The IMEI lets police flag the device and is required for any insurance claim.

Lock and wipe remotely via Apple's Find My or Google's Find My Device.

## If the scam involved drink-spiking or physical harm

Go to a hospital before you go to the police. Toxicology tests have a short window. A list of 24/7 hospitals with English-speaking staff in each city is in Appendix C.

**Emergency numbers in {COUNTRY}:**
- **{NUMBER}** — {POLICE}
- **{NUMBER}** — ambulance / medical emergency
- **{NUMBER}** — fire
- **{NUMBER}** — {other country-specific line}

Your embassy can help coordinate English-speaking medical care and, if your passport was involved, coordinate a replacement.
